i 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068569 #edb/ref 1069 "e-466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068569 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068569 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068570 #edb/ref 1069 "e-467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068570 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068570 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1039 "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068915 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/